  "lesson": "L20",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>A LoRA fine-tune with rank 1 plateaus at a training loss well above full fine-tuning's, while rank 16 matches it. What is the best explanation?</p>",
  "answer": "a",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>The update the task needs has more than one important direction, and a rank-1 adapter can only represent one.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>Rank 1 needs a larger learning rate, and nothing else.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>Rank 1 overfits.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>The adapter was initialized wrongly.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>A rank-<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span></span></span></span> adapter can only produce rank-<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span></span></span></span> updates; by Eckart-Young its best possible loss is set by the singular values it cannot reach. More steps cannot fix that. Raise the rank, or adapt more matrices, and compare against full fine-tuning to see how much is left.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L20/concept1",
  "lesson": "L20",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why is the prompt masked out of the fine-tuning loss?",
  "answer": "The model's job is the response; training on prompt tokens spends the gradient on reproducing inputs and teaches the model to generate prompts.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L20/concept2",
  "lesson": "L20",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What does LoRA train?",
  "answer": "A low-rank update (alpha / r) B A added to each frozen weight, with B starting at zero; it trains r (d_in + d_out) parameters per matrix and can be merged into the weight afterwards.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L20/concept3",
  "lesson": "L20",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why is a low-rank update usually enough for fine-tuning?",
  "answer": "Fine-tuning updates have quickly decaying singular values, so by Eckart-Young a truncated SVD of low rank captures most of the update.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L20/concept4",
  "lesson": "L20",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What is catastrophic forgetting, and how is it limited?",
  "answer": "The loss on the pretraining distribution rises as fine-tuning moves the weights; small learning rates, replaying pretraining data, a penalty toward the pretrained weights and training fewer parameters limit it.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L20/concept5",
  "lesson": "L20",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why does distillation use a temperature, and why the T squared factor?",
  "answer": "Softening both distributions exposes the teacher's small probabilities on wrong answers; the gradient of the softened KL scales as 1/T, so T squared keeps its size independent of T.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L20/concept6",
  "lesson": "L20",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "How do you pack several examples into one sequence correctly?",
  "answer": "Use an attention mask that only lets positions attend within their own example, and restart the position ids at each example.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
